 - **When the result goes.** At the next `pointerdown` outside it, of any pointer type and button, and at no other press: a passive listener on the `document` in the capture phase, added in the commit that shows the result and removed when it ends or the canvas unmounts, so the outside press then goes on untouched to what it hit, and a press on the result itself leaves it. It also goes when the panel returns (an Undo of the spacing), when a new draft is loaded (a new `generation`, whether or not it is a pile), and when the canvas unmounts. Other edits, an Undo or Redo that leaves the draft not a pile, keys, wheel, the fit and autosave leave it as it was. A Redo of the spacing shows none. There is no timer. This listener is not a second gate: it is on the document, exists only while the result shows and changes nothing; the canvas's one `mouseup` window listener stays the only one.
 - **What it costs, and what it does not claim.** The area the panel covered stays covered, and the cards the fit places there stay hidden, until the next press elsewhere: at 512 px that is up to 392 × 129.5 px and includes **Zoom Out**. The owner's decision stands that Space cards is explicit and undoable; this keeps the panel's footprint, not an invisible one. The box is fixed at the size measured when the button was pressed. A resize, a panel or toolbar toggle or a change of text size while it shows may move or shrink it, and nothing here protects what lies under its old place then, after the press that ended it, or after a load; the presses of the same browser-counted run that follow a deliberate press elsewhere are not covered either. Which element a native press lands on is a browser fact that jsdom does not have.
 - **Undo and redo.** Undo restores the exact earlier draft, including absent `ui`, and the offer returns, taking the result away (a Redo leaves none). Redo restores the spaced draft object itself, without computing it again. Both are ordinary edits that autosave sends.
